.TH SCOPELINK-SIMULATOR 1 "2026-08-12" "indi-scopelink 1.0" "INDI Drivers"
.SH NAME
scopelink-simulator \- serve a simulated ScopeLink controller on a pseudo terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scopelink-simulator
.RB [ \-\-generation
.IR 2 | 3 ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scopelink-simulator
answers the ScopeLink wire protocol on a pseudo terminal, so that anything which expects a serial port
can be exercised without a controller attached. It prints the path of the pseudo terminal it created and
then serves one client until it is stopped.
.PP
Use it when the thing being tested is a port:
.BR scopelink-cli (1),
a client's port handling, or the driver's own serial path. To try the
.B driver
without hardware there is no need for this program at all \- turn the driver's
.B SIMULATION
switch on instead, which connects it to the same simulated controller directly. See
.BR indi_scopelink (1).
.PP
It is not a model of the firmware. It answers every command the driver sends, with frame layouts taken
from the same measurements the driver's parsers are built on, and it moves its motors at a plausible
rate so that motion handling has something to follow. The values it reports are plausible rather than
measured.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-generation " 2|3"
Hardware generation to imitate. Defaults to 3. The generations differ in what they offer, not only in
what they report: generation 2 has no USB hub and no smart switch diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
.B scopelink-simulator --generation 3
ScopeLink simulator, hardware generation 3
Port: /dev/pts/7

.B scopelink-cli --port /dev/pts/7 info
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR indi_scopelink (1),
.BR scopelink-cli (1)
